# Strict !important Prevention Rules

## Purpose and Scope

This rule enforces a complete prohibition on the use of `!important` in CSS declarations. The `!important` declaration breaks CSS cascade and inheritance, creates maintenance nightmares, and indicates poor CSS architecture. Instead, developers must use proper CSS specificity, CSS Modules scoping, and CSS custom properties to achieve the desired styling outcomes.

## Standards and Requirements

### Absolute Prohibition
- **NEVER** use `!important` in any CSS declaration
- **ANY** `!important` usage should trigger immediate refactoring
- Use proper CSS specificity and cascade instead

### Why !important is Forbidden
- Breaks CSS cascade and inheritance
- Makes debugging extremely difficult
- Creates maintenance nightmares
- Indicates poor CSS architecture
- Conflicts with CSS Modules scoping benefits

### Alternative Patterns

#### 1. Increase Specificity Naturally
```css
/* BAD - using !important */
.button {
  background: blue !important;
}

/* GOOD - increase specificity */
.component .button {
  background: blue;
}

/* BETTER - use CSS Modules scoping */
.button {
  background: var(--color-primary);
}
```

#### 2. CSS Modules Automatic Scoping
```css
/* CSS Modules automatically scope classes */
/* No need for !important - scoping prevents conflicts */

.card {
  background: white; /* Scoped to this component only */
  padding: var(--spacing-md);
}

.cardTitle {
  color: var(--color-primary); /* No conflicts possible */
}
```

#### 3. CSS Custom Properties for Overrides
```css
/* Base component styles */
.button {
  background: var(--button-bg, var(--color-primary));
  color: var(--button-color, white);
  padding: var(--button-padding, var(--spacing-sm) var(--spacing-md));
}

/* Variant override using custom properties */
.primaryButton {
  --button-bg: var(--color-primary);
  --button-color: white;
}

.secondaryButton {
  --button-bg: var(--color-secondary);
  --button-color: var(--color-text);
}
```

#### 4. Component State Classes
```css
/* State-based styling without !important */
.card {
  background: var(--color-background);
  transform: translateY(0);
  transition: all var(--transition-speed) ease;
}

.card:hover {
  background: var(--color-background-hover);
  transform: translateY(-2px);
}

.cardActive {
  background: var(--color-primary);
  color: white;
}
```

#### 5. Proper CSS Cascade Order
```css
/* Order matters - later rules override earlier ones */

/* Base styles */
.button {
  background: var(--color-neutral);
  color: var(--color-text);
}

/* State styles come after base */
.button:hover {
  background: var(--color-neutral-hover);
}

.button:focus {
  background: var(--color-neutral-focus);
  outline: 2px solid var(--color-primary);
}

/* Variant styles come last */
.buttonPrimary {
  background: var(--color-primary);
  color: white;
}

.buttonPrimary:hover {
  background: var(--color-primary-hover);
}
```

## Examples

### Good Examples

```css
/* Using CSS Modules scoping */
.beerCard {
  background: var(--color-background);
  border-radius: var(--border-radius);
}

.beerCardTitle {
  color: var(--color-primary);
  font-size: var(--font-size-lg);
}
```

```css
/* Using CSS custom properties for variants */
.button {
  background: var(--button-bg, var(--color-primary));
  color: var(--button-color, white);
  padding: var(--button-padding, var(--spacing-sm) var(--spacing-md));
}

.buttonSecondary {
  --button-bg: var(--color-secondary);
  --button-color: var(--color-text);
}
```

```css
/* Proper cascade order */
.element {
  display: flex;
}

@media (max-width: 768px) {
  .element {
    display: block;
  }
}
```

### Bad Examples

```css
.button {
  background: blue !important;
}
```
*Fails: Uses !important which breaks CSS cascade and makes debugging difficult.*

```css
@media (max-width: 768px) {
  .element {
    display: block !important;
  }
}
```
*Fails: Uses !important for responsive design when proper cascade would work.*

```css
.utility {
  display: none !important;
}
```
*Fails: Uses !important for utility classes when CSS Modules scoping prevents conflicts.*

## Implementation Guidelines

### Refactoring !important Issues

#### Common Scenario 1: Overriding Third-Party Styles
```css
/* BAD - fighting with !important */
.myComponent {
  color: red !important;
}

/* GOOD - use CSS Modules scoping + specificity */
.myComponent {
  color: var(--color-error);
}

/* If third-party lib uses !important, wrap in higher specificity */
.componentWrapper .myComponent {
  color: var(--color-error);
}
```

#### Common Scenario 2: Media Query Overrides
```css
/* BAD - using !important for responsive */
.element {
  display: flex;
}

@media (max-width: 768px) {
  .element {
    display: block !important;
  }
}

/* GOOD - proper cascade */
.element {
  display: flex;
}

@media (max-width: 768px) {
  .element {
    display: block;
  }
}
```

#### Common Scenario 3: Utility Override
```css
/* BAD - utility with !important */
.hidden {
  display: none !important;
}

/* GOOD - CSS Module utility */
.visuallyHidden {
  position: absolute;
  width: 1px;
  height: 1px;
  padding: 0;
  margin: -1px;
  overflow: hidden;
  clip: rect(0, 0, 0, 0);
  white-space: nowrap;
  border: 0;
}

.hidden {
  display: none;
}
```

### CSS Modules Specificity Benefits
- Automatically scoped classes prevent conflicts
- No need to fight specificity wars
- Clean, predictable cascade
- Easy to reason about and debug

### Debugging Without !important
```css
/* Use browser dev tools to inspect specificity */
/* Check the computed styles panel */
/* Look for overridden rules */

/* Add temporary background to see element boundaries */
.debugElement {
  background: red; /* Remove after debugging */
  border: 1px solid blue; /* Remove after debugging */
}
```

### Code Quality Checks
- [ ] No `!important` declarations anywhere in CSS
- [ ] Proper specificity used instead
- [ ] CSS Modules scoping leveraged
- [ ] CSS custom properties used for variants
- [ ] Cascade order respected
- [ ] Media queries don't require !important
- [ ] Third-party overrides use proper specificity

### Automated Prevention
Consider adding a linter rule to catch !important usage:
```json
// .stylelintrc.json
{
  "rules": {
    "declaration-no-important": true
  }
}
```

### Emergency !important Guidelines
If you absolutely must use !important (extremely rare):
1. Document why it's necessary
2. Create a TODO to refactor it
3. Use CSS custom properties to make it overridable
4. Limit scope to single property

```css
/* EMERGENCY ONLY - with documentation */
.emergencyOverride {
  /* TODO: Refactor this !important usage */
  /* Reason: Third-party library forces inline styles */
  display: block !important;
}
```

